{"page": 35, "text": "\u2022 North America On-Highway end market net sales increased $135 million, or 41%, principally driven by higher demand for medium-duty trucks and class 8 vocational trucks, price increases on certain products and increased demand for hybrid transit buses. \u2022 Service Parts, Support Equipment and Other end market net sales increased $22 million, or 14%, principally driven by price increases on certain products. \u2022 Defense end market net sales increased $16 million, or 21%, principally driven by increased demand for Tracked vehicle applications, price increases on certain products and the continued execution of our growth initiatives. \u2022 Global Off-Highway net sales increased $8 million, or 114%, principally driven by higher demand from the energy sector outside North America. \u2022 Outside North America On-Highway end market net sales increased $7 million, or 6%, principally driven by increased demand in Europe.\n36\nTable of Contents\nCost of sales\nCost of sales for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was $1,029 million compared to $363 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 183%. $555 million of cost of goods sold was attributable to Allison Off-Highway, including $10 million of depreciation expense related to the stepped-up basis in property, plant and equipment. The remaining increase of $111 million was principally driven by higher direct material and manufacturing expense commensurate with increased net sales, unfavorable direct material costs and higher incentive compensation expense in Allison Transmission.\nGross profit\nGross profit for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was $505 million compared to $330 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 53%. $98 million of the increase in gross profit was attributable to Allison Off-Highway, while gross profit attributable to Allison Transmission increased $77 million, principally driven by higher volume in the North America On-Highway end market and $39 million of price increases on certain products, partially offset by higher direct material and manufacturing expense commensurate with increased net sales, unfavorable direct material costs and higher incentive compensation expense. Gross profit as a percent of net sales for the three months ended September 30, 2026 decreased 14.7 percentage points compared to the same period in 2025 principally driven by the addition of Allison Off-Highway, the products of which have a lower average gross profit as a percent of net sales profile compared to Allison Transmission products.\nSelling, general and administrative\n"}
{"page": 36, "text": "Selling, general and administrative expenses for the three months ended September 30, 2026 were $142 million compared to $83 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 71%. Selling, general and administrative expenses of $52 million were attributable to Allison Off-Highway, including $20 million of amortization expense for intangible assets recognized from the Acquisition. The remaining increase was principally driven by increased incentive compensation expense, partially offset by decreased expenses related to the Acquisition.\nEngineering \u2014 research and development\nEngineering expenses for the three months ended September 30, 2026 were $59 million compared to $43 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 37%. The increase was primarily due to engineering expenses of $14 million attributable to Allison Off-Highway.\nInterest expense, net\nInterest expense, net for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was $51 million compared to $24 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 113%. The increase was principally driven by $17 million of interest expense related to the Incremental Term Loan, $7 million of interest expense related to the 5.875% Senior Notes 2033 and $6 million of lower interest income.\nOther (expense) income, net\nOther (expense) income, net for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was other expense of ($15) million compared to other income of $2 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The change was principally driven by $17 million of restructuring charges related to Allison Off-Highway footprint optimization activities.\nIncome tax expense\nIncome tax expense for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was $47 million, resulting in an effective tax rate of 20%, compared to $45 million of income tax expense and an effective tax rate of 25% for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The increase in income tax expense was principally driven by higher income\n37\nTable of Contents\nbefore income taxes, partially offset by a lower effective tax rate. The decrease in the effective tax rate was primarily driven by impacts related to the One Big Beautiful Bill Act enacted in 2025.\n38\nTable of Contents\nAllison Consolidated Comparison of the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025\nThe following table sets forth certain financial information for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025. The following table and discussion should be read in conjunction with the information contained in our condensed consolidated financial statements and the notes thereto included in Part I, Item 1 of this Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q.\nNine Months Ended September 30,\n(unaudited, dollars in millions)\n2026\n%\nof net sales\n2025\n%\nof net sales\nNet sales\n$\n4,506\n100\n%\n$\n2,273\n100\n%\nCost of sales\n3,080\n68\n1,162\n51\nGross Profit\n1,426\n32\n1,111\n49\nOperating Expenses:\nSelling, general and administrative\n467\n10\n274\n12\n"}
{"page": 37, "text": "Engineering \u2014 research and development\n169\n4\n128\n6\nTotal Operating Expenses\n636\n14\n402\n18\nOperating Income\n790\n18\n709\n31\nInterest expense, net\n(166\n)\n(4\n)\n(67\n)\n(3\n)\nOther (expense) income, net\n(26\n)\n(1\n)\n15\n1\nIncome before income taxes\n598\n13\n657\n29\nIncome tax expense\n(114\n)\n(2\n)\n(133\n)\n(6\n)\nNet income\n$\n484\n11\n%\n$\n524\n23\n%\nNet sales\nThe Allison Off-Highway segment generated $2,032 million of net sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026.\nThe Allison Transmission segment generated $2,474 million of net sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026. Net sales increased $201 million, or 9%, for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025.\nThe increase was principally driven by the following:\n\u2022 North America On-Highway end market net sales increased $88 million, or 7%, principally driven by higher demand for medium-duty trucks and class 8 vocational trucks, price increases on certain products and increased demand for hybrid transit buses. \u2022 Defense end market net sales increased $86 million, or 44%, principally driven by increased demand for Tracked vehicle applications, price increases on certain products and the continued execution of our growth initiatives. \u2022 Service Parts, Support Equipment and Other end market net sales increased $28 million, or 6%, principally driven by price increases on certain products. \u2022 Global Off-Highway end market net sales increased $4 million, or 10%, principally driven by increased demand from the global energy sector, partially offset by lower demand from the global mining and construction sectors.\nThese increases were partially offset by the following:\n\u2022 Outside North America On-Highway end market net sales decreased $5 million, or 1%, principally driven by lower demand in Asia and South America, partially offset by price increases on certain products and higher demand in Europe.\n39\nTable of Contents\nCost of sales\nCost of sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $3,080 million compared to $1,162 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 165%. $1,766 million of cost of goods sold was attributable to Allison Off-Highway, including purchase price accounting allocations of $64 million of expense related to the stepped-up basis in inventory and $41 million of depreciation expense related to the stepped-up basis in property, plant and equipment. The remaining $152 million increase was principally driven by higher direct material and manufacturing expense commensurate with increased net sales, unfavorable direct material costs and higher incentive compensation expense in Allison Transmission.\nGross profit\n"}
{"page": 38, "text": "Gross profit for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $1,426 million compared to $1,111 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 28%. $266 million of the increase in gross profit was attributable to Allison Off-Highway, while the gross profit attributable to Allison Transmission increased $49 million, principally driven by $96 million of price increases on certain products and increased net sales in the North America On-Highway and Defense end markets, partially offset by higher direct material and manufacturing expense commensurate with increased net sales, unfavorable direct material costs and higher incentive compensation expense. Gross profit as a percent of net sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 decreased 17.3 percentage points compared to the same period in 2025 principally driven by the addition of Allison Off-Highway, the products of which have a lower average gross profit as a percent of net sales profile compared to Allison Transmission products.\nSelling, general and administrative\nSelling, general and administrative expenses were $467 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $274 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 70%. Selling, general and administrative expenses of $164 million were attributable to Allison Off-Highway, including $61 million of amortization expense for intangible assets recognized from the Acquisition. The remaining increase of $29 million was principally driven by higher incentive compensation expense and increased commercial activities spending, partially offset by decreased expenses related to the Acquisition and lower product warranty expense.\nEngineering \u2014 research and development\nEngineering expenses for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 were $169 million compared to $128 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 32%. Engineering expenses of $44 million were attributable to Allison Off-Highway. The remaining decrease was principally driven by decreased product initiatives spending in Allison Transmission, partially offset by higher incentive compensation expense.\nInterest expense, net\nInterest expense, net for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $166 million compared to $67 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of 148%. The increase was principally driven by $49 million of interest expense related to the Incremental Term Loan, $22 million of interest expense related to the 5.875% Senior Notes 2033 and $17 million of lower interest income.\nOther (expense) income, net\n"}
{"page": 39, "text": "Other (expense) income, net for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was other expense of ($26) million compared to other income of $15 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The change was principally driven by a $22 million change in unrealized mark-to-market adjustments for marketable securities and $17 million of restructuring charges related to Allison Off-Highway footprint optimization activities.\n40\nTable of Contents\nIncome tax expense\nIncome tax expense for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $114 million, resulting in an effective tax rate of 19%, compared to $133 million of income tax expense and an effective tax rate of 20% for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in income tax expense was principally driven by lower taxable income.\n41\nTable of Contents\nNon-GAAP Financial Measures\nWe use Adjusted Earnings before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation, and Amortization (\u201cEBITDA\u201d) and Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales to measure our operating profitability. We believe that Adjusted EBITDA and Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales provide management, investors and creditors with useful measures of the operational results of our business and increase the period-to-period comparability of our operating profitability and comparability with other companies. Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales is also used in the calculation of management\u2019s incentive compensation program. The most directly comparable U.S. generally accepted accounting principles (\u201cGAAP\u201d) measures to Adjusted EBITDA and Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales are Net income and Net income as a percent of net sales, respectively. Adjusted EBITDA is calculated as earnings before interest expense, net, income tax expense, amortization of intangible assets, depreciation of property, plant and equipment and other adjustments as defined by the Credit Agreement governing ATI's term loan facility in the amount of $505 million due March 2031 (\u201cTerm Loan\u201d), the Incremental Term Loan and the Revolving Credit Facility (together, the \"Senior Secured Credit Facility\"). Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales is calculated as Adjusted EBITDA divided by net sales.\n"}
{"page": 40, "text": "In addition, we believe Adjusted net income, Adjusted basic earnings per share attributable to common stockholders (\"Adjusted basic EPS\") and Adjusted diluted earnings per share attributable to common stockholders (\"Adjusted diluted EPS\") provide management, investors and creditors with useful measures of our core business performance and trends and increase the period-to-period comparability of our results of operations. The most directly comparable GAAP measures to Adjusted net income, Adjusted basic EPS and Adjusted diluted EPS are Net income, Basic earnings per share attributable to common stockholders (\"Basic EPS\") and Diluted earnings per share attributable to common stockholders (\"Diluted EPS\"), respectively. Adjusted net income is calculated as net income excluding the effect of certain non-cash, non-recurring, infrequent or unusual items such as: amortization related to acquired intangible assets, depreciation of the stepped-up basis in property, plant and equipment related to the Acquisition, stepped-up basis in inventory related to the Acquisition, stock-based compensation expense, Acquisition-related expenses, impairment charges, other one-off adjustments and the tax effect of the adjustments. Adjusted basic EPS and Adjusted diluted EPS are calculated by dividing Adjusted net income by the weighted average shares of common stock outstanding and diluted weighted average shares of common stock outstanding, respectively.\nWe use Adjusted free cash flow to evaluate the amount of cash generated by our business that, after the capital investment needed to maintain and grow our business and certain mandatory debt service requirements, can be used for repayment of debt, stockholder distributions and strategic opportunities, including investing in our business. We believe that Adjusted free cash flow enhances the understanding of the cash flows of our business for management, investors and creditors. Adjusted free cash flow is also used in the calculation of management\u2019s incentive compensation program. The most directly comparable GAAP measure to Adjusted free cash flow is Net cash provided by operating activities. Adjusted free cash flow is calculated as Net cash provided by operating activities after additions of long-lived assets.\n42\nTable of Contents\nThe following is a reconciliation of Net income to Adjusted EBITDA, Net income as a percent of net sales to Adjusted EBITDA as a percent of net sales, and Net cash provided by operating activities to Adjusted free cash flow:\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n(unaudited, dollars in millions)\n2026\n2025\n2026\n2025\nNet income (GAAP)\n$\n191\n$\n137\n$\n484\n$\n524\nplus:\nInterest expense, net\n51\n24\n166\n67\nDepreciation of property, plant and equipment\n46\n30\n136\n87\nIncome tax expense\n47\n45\n114\n133\nAmortization of intangible assets\n22\n2\n66\n5\nRecognition of stepped-up basis in inventory (a)\n1\n\u2014\n64\n\u2014\nDepreciation related to stepped-up basis in assets (b)\n10\n\u2014\n41\n\u2014\n"}
